    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "The starting amount is the value at x = 0. For V(x) = 8000 · (1/2)ˣ, V(0) = 8000. Then 4000, 2000, 1000, … — halving toward zero, but never quite reaching it.",
      "figure": "exp-v0-8000"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Read the start.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "For V(x) = 8000 · (1/2)^x, what is the starting value V(0)?",
        "answer": 8000,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 4000,
            "feedback": "That's V(1), after one step. At x = 0, (1/2)⁰ = 1, so V(0) = 8000."
          },
          {
            "value": 1,
            "feedback": "(1/2)⁰ = 1, but you still multiply by a: V(0) = 8000."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "V(0) = 8000 · (1/2)⁰ = 8000."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Decay by other factors works the same way. A value that keeps 1/3 each step from 729 is V(x) = 729 · (1/3)ˣ: 729, 243, 81, … The decay factor sets how fast it falls.",
      "figure": "exp-keep-third"
    },
    {
      "id": "i3",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Thirds this time.",
      "widget": {
